# Methods
#
# The justification vocabulary. One record per method. A step's justification
# line names exactly one of these, or cites an item, which is the `citation`
# record below.
#
#   form        the justification line as it is written
#   block       none, or required when the method is justified by a subproof
#   parts       the part markers the block carries, in order
#   part-opens  what a part marker is followed by, when it carries an assumption
#   checks      what a reader checks, from SYNTAX.md
#   metamath    the lemma families the expansion targets
#   first-used  the pilot that introduced it
#   note        anything a reader or an implementer needs
#
# Every method must eventually carry a specified expansion, as READERS.md
# requires. None is written yet. `checks` is what the acceptance test needs.

method citation
  form        def:X v := t, from L
              thm:X v := t, from L
  block       none
  checks      apply the definition or theorem X with its variables set as given,
              using the lines L to discharge its hypotheses. The claim is the
              literal instance of one sentence of X's conclusion.
  metamath    the cited item's own label, with substitution
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        The method name is not written. The pointer is the justification.
              The prefix must match the kind of the item it names.

method obtain
  form        obtain a, b: item, from L
              obtain a, from L
              obtain a from line L
  block       none
  checks      the cited item, or with no item the line in L, concludes an
              existence claim. Name its objects a and b. The claim is the body.
  metamath    exlimiv, eximd, rexlimiv
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        obtain keeps its names, because a new name is a choice the reader
              cannot infer.

method exhibit
  form        exhibit, from L
  block       none
  checks      the claim is a bare "there is" sentence. The lines L state its body
              with some value in place of the bound variable, and the reader
              finds the value by comparing.
  metamath    rspcev, spcev
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        The value is never written. By the literal-instance rule the cited
              lines determine it. Where the "there is" is a sentence of a cited
              definition, no keyword is used and the citation stands alone.

method substitute
  form        substitute e (line L1) into line L2
              substitute e (line L1)
  block       none
  checks      with `into`, the claim is line L2 with one side of the equation e,
              which is part of line L1, replaced by the other. Without `into`,
              the claim is t = t′, where t′ is t with one side of e replaced by
              the other.
  metamath    oveq1d, oveq2d, eqtrd, fveq2d
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        Writes "right to left" where the direction matters.

method instantiate
  form        instantiate v := t in line L, from L2
  block       none
  checks      line L claims "for every v ∈ X, B". The claim is B with t in place
              of v, and L2 supplies t ∈ X. Several variables may be given at
              once. L may also be a hypothesis or supposition label, or a
              definition whose sentence is a "for every".
  metamath    rspcv, rspccva
  first-used  bezout

method algebra
  form        algebra, from L
  block       none
  checks      ring and field identities, starting from the equations in L, in the
              variables and in named subterms treated as opaque; and a
              disequality that is a rearrangement of a cited one.
  decides     equality of rational expressions over a field, with every subterm
              that is not built from numerals, + − · / and a numeral power
              treated as an atom it knows nothing about. Also a disequality
              e ≠ 0, when e is a nonzero rational multiple of a cited d ≠ 0;
              not one that is anything else, which would be a fact about the
              field rather than an identity of it.
  hypotheses  every atom is a real number, and every denominator is nonzero;
              both are dull facts discharged by a cited line or a requires line
  specified-in METHODS.md
  metamath    a normaliser over the ℂ field lemmas
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        Turns (1 − a^(k + 1))/(1 − a) + a^(k + 1) into
              (1 − a^(k + 1)·a)/(1 − a), but will not turn a^(k + 1)·a into
              a^(k + 2), because the exponent is not a numeral and so the power
              is an atom. Anything about what an atom means is a cited theorem
              in a step of its own.

method arithmetic
  form        arithmetic
  block       none
  checks      a fact about closed numerals: value, order, disequality, or
              membership in ℕ ℕ₀ ℤ ℚ ℝ.
  decides     relations between closed numeral expressions, by evaluating each
              side to a rational in lowest terms
  hypotheses  none, and it cites nothing; requires lines do not nest, so this
              is where that recursion stops
  specified-in METHODS.md
  metamath    2re, 2z, 0z, 1z, 1nn, 2pos, 2ne0, 1lt2, and the deccl and decadd
              families for longer numerals
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        The line between arithmetic and algebra is closed numerals against
              symbols: a claim containing a variable is never arithmetic.

method membership
  form        membership, from L
  block       none
  checks      that the term is in the number system because its parts are,
              starting from L.
  decides     closure of a number system under the operations at the head of
              the term, down to atoms whose membership L says
  hypotheses  a divisor is not zero, by a cited line, a requires line, or
              being a digit other than zero or a product of such
  specified-in METHODS.md
  metamath    readdcld, remulcld, redivcld, divcld, mulne0d, divne0d
  first-used  triangular-reciprocals
  note        What algebra and inequalities work out for every atom they
              touch, stated as a step's claim, so that a membership is one
              line rather than a library item per operation.

method inequalities
  form        inequalities, from L
  block       none
  checks      the rules for inequalities, starting from L.
  decides     linear arithmetic over an ordered field, with every subterm that
              is not built from numerals and + − · / treated as an atom it
              knows nothing about
  hypotheses  every atom is a real number, discharged by a cited line or a
              requires line like any other dull fact
  specified-in METHODS.md
  metamath    ltne, lelttr, ltnle, letri3
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        The name misleads. This is about the ordering of numbers, not
              about things being unequal. A ≠ reaches it only because the
              order gives one a meaning — a ≠ b is a < b or b < a — so a
              disequality is this method's when the order is what settles it,
              as 0 ≤ r with r ≠ 0 giving 0 < r, and algebra's when no order
              is involved, as 1 − a ≠ 0 from a ≠ 1.

method join
  form        join L
  block       none
  checks      the claim is the sentences of the cited lines put together; with
              one cited line it is that line.
  decides     nothing. It combines what the cited lines already say and infers
              no new fact, which is why it is the one method named for what it
              does with its arguments rather than for a body of knowledge or a
              shape of argument
  hypotheses  none
  specified-in METHODS.md
  metamath    jca, and the projection lemmas where a cited line contributes one
              of several sentences
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        Named `lines` until it was specified. That name said what it
              cited and nothing about what it did, and four unrelated jobs
              collected under it unnoticed: joining, double negation,
              disjunctive syllogism and restating a case assumption. The two
              laws are now thm:stdlib/reasoning/double-negation and thm:stdlib/reasoning/disjunctive-syllogism.
              Restating one cited line is joining a list of one.

method contradiction
  form        contradiction
  block       required
  part-opens  suppose B (S)
  checks      C is the step's claim, and the block opens by supposing B,
              labelled S. One of two things holds and neither is in doubt,
              since no formula is its own double negation: B is "not C", which
              is reductio, or C is "not B", which proves a negation directly.
              The block's last step states some P and also not P. Any
              rewriting of B is a step inside the block. See METHODS.md.
  metamath    pm2.65, and notnotr as well where the claim is not a negation
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational

method fix
  form        fix
  block       required
  part-opens  let x ∈ S (K), one or more of them, and assume A (H)
  checks      the claim is "for every x ∈ S, ..., if A then B", one "for every"
              per let line and in that order. The block opens with the claim's
              let and assume lines, each labelled, and its last step is B.
  metamath    ralrimiva, ex, alrimiv
  first-used  sum-formula
  note        The same rule by which a theorem's block proves its statement. At
              theorem level no method is named because the let and assume lines
              are the statement.

              More than one let is Bezout's step 10, which fixes an x and a y
              and supposes their combination is a natural number. Each let is
              given back by its own ralrimiva, innermost first, and the
              supposition by ex before any of them.

method induction
  form        induction on n starting at m, from H
  block       required
  parts       base, step
  checks      the claim is P(n), where H gives n ∈ ℕ or n ∈ ℕ₀ and, if m is above
              the set's first element, n ≥ m. The base part's last step claims
              P(m). The step part's last step claims "for every k ∈ ℤ with
              k ≥ m, if P(k) then P(k + 1)", with P read off the claim.
  metamath    nnind, nn0ind, uzind
  first-used  sum-formula
  note        The starting point is written even when the set fixes it, so that
              every induction reads the same way and inductions from 2 or 4 need
              no new form.

method cases
  form        cases, from L
  block       required
  parts       case
  parts-repeat one per disjunct of L, in the order of L
  part-opens  assume, labelled
  checks      L claims "P or Q". Each part opens with assume of its disjunct,
              labelled, in the order of L, and its last step claims the same
              formula as the step above the block.
  metamath    jaodan, jaoi
  first-used  triangle-inequality
  note        A case that is impossible still ends by claiming the common
              formula: it reaches some P and not P, then claims the formula by
              thm:stdlib/reasoning/from-contradiction.

method calculation
  form        calculation, then a chain
  block       required
  checks      each line of the chain is `rel t  L`, where rel is =, ≤ or < and L
              is one cited line whose claim is exactly the previous term rel t.
              The claim is first term rel last term, with rel being = if every
              line is =, ≤ if every line is = or ≤, and < if any line is <.
  metamath    eqtrd, 3eqtrd, eqbrtrd, lelttrd
  first-used  sqrt2-irrational
  note        A calculation only joins. Every line cites a numbered step or
              label that states that line's relation, and all reasoning is in
              those steps. A chain never carries a method on a line. A line may
              read a cited equation right to left, and writes "right to left"
              when it does.
